{
  "id": "020-stroop-test",
  "title": "Say the colour, not the word",
  "format": "short",
  "theme": "psychology",
  "voice": "af_heart",
  "speed": 1.0,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Try this. Say the colour of each word out loud. Not the word. The colour.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "stroop",
        "items": [
          [
            "red",
            "blue"
          ],
          [
            "green",
            "red"
          ],
          [
            "blue",
            "yellow"
          ],
          [
            "yellow",
            "green"
          ],
          [
            "purple",
            "orange"
          ],
          [
            "orange",
            "purple"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "colour."
      ],
      "pause": 4.0
    },
    {
      "text": "Harder than it should be, right?",
      "visual": "same",
      "emphasis": [
        "Harder"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It's called the Stroop effect, published by psychologist John Ridley Stroop in 1935.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "timeline",
        "points": [
          {
            "year": "1929",
            "label": "first reported in Germany"
          },
          {
            "year": "1935",
            "label": "Stroop publishes"
          }
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "Stroop"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Your brain reads words automatically. You can't switch it off.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "brain"
        ],
        "label": "reading is automatic"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "automatically."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "So the word arrives first, and fights with the colour you're trying to name.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "split",
        "left": "the word",
        "right": "the colour"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "fights"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Overriding it takes real effort. That's the slowdown you just felt.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "words",
        "words": [
          "brain",
          "conflict"
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "effort."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Psychologists still use this test to measure attention and focus.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "icon",
        "names": [
          "eye"
        ],
        "label": "a test of attention"
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "attention"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Go back and try again. Faster this time.",
      "visual": {
        "type": "stroop",
        "items": [
          [
            "blue",
            "red"
          ],
          [
            "yellow",
            "blue"
          ],
          [
            "red",
            "green"
          ],
          [
            "orange",
            "yellow"
          ],
          [
            "green",
            "purple"
          ],
          [
            "purple",
            "orange"
          ]
        ]
      },
      "emphasis": [
        "Faster"
      ],
      "pause": 2.5
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "claim": "J. Ridley Stroop published the effect in 1935 (Jaensch reported it in 1929); naming ink colour of a conflicting colour word is slower because reading is automatic",
      "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stroop_effect"
    }
  ],
